          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the olive oil, garlic, rosemary, lemon zest, juice, chilli if using, and black pepper in a bowl. Rub this marinade all over the spatchcocked chicken, including under the skin. Leave for at least 30 minutes, or overnight in the fridge."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a large cast iron skillet over medium-high heat. Scrape most of the marinade off the skin side to prevent burning."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the chicken skin-side down in the hot pan. Set a second heavy pan or foil-wrapped weight directly on top. Cook for 25 minutes without lifting the weight."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the weight. The skin should be deep golden and very crisp. Flip the chicken and cook for a further 10 to 15 minutes until the juices run clear at the thigh joint, or an instant-read thermometer reads 75C at the thickest point."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest for 5 minutes before carving. Serve with the pan juices spooned over."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Skin burning before the chicken is cooked through"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The heat is too high. Reduce to medium and continue. The weight keeps the skin in contact, so it will colour without needing high heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Skin sticking to the pan"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Do not try to move it. Stuck skin means it is not yet ready to release. Wait another 2 minutes and it will lift cleanly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Inside still pink after flipping"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cover loosely with foil and return to medium heat for another 5 to 10 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pollo al mattone is a Tuscan farmhouse dish. It uses a technique as old as Italian cooking itself. Florentine restaurants serve it alongside bistecca. The pressed brick is now often a cast iron pan, but the principle has not changed. Simple, fast, and very satisfying."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
